"""Additional criterion-specific worked examples for the Operable lessons."""

CASES = {
'2.1.1': '''Consider a file manager where a pointer user can select a file, open its context menu, rename it, and confirm the new name. Follow that entire sequence using only a keyboard. A control can receive focus but still fail if its activation handler listens only for mouse events. Native buttons and links give a sound starting point, while custom composite controls also need the expected arrow-key behavior and clear instructions. After renaming, verify that the updated name is available and that focus has not disappeared. A drawing function may qualify for the path-dependent exception only when its underlying operation truly depends on the path; the exception does not cover unrelated controls such as saving, selecting a color, or opening a menu.''',
'2.1.2': '''A calendar popup can appear to be a keyboard trap even when its initial controls are accessible. Enter the date field, open the calendar, move through the available dates, and attempt to leave without choosing one. If Escape closes the popup, check where focus lands and whether the next Tab moves on to the next field. If the interface uses a nonstandard exit mechanism, tell users what it is before they become stuck. A modal dialog may intentionally constrain focus to its controls while open, but it needs a keyboard-operable route out; a close button that can only be clicked does not solve the problem.''',
'2.1.3': '''Imagine a diagram editor where creating a connection requires dragging a line from one node to another. A keyboard alternative could let a user select the source node, choose an add-connection command, select the destination, and edit the resulting line. Evaluate more than initial creation: users may also need to move endpoints, change labels, remove connections, and save the diagram. This enhanced criterion removes the path-dependent exception in the Level A keyboard criterion. Do not infer that a simple keyboard shortcut for opening the editor covers the drawing operations themselves. Check the complete set of functions and compare their results across input methods.''',
'2.1.4': '''A video player uses the letter M to mute sound. If the shortcut is registered on the entire document, it may fire when a person dictates a sentence or types into a nearby comment field. Choose one of the permitted safeguards: disable the shortcut, allow the user to remap it with a non-printable modifier, or make it active only when the relevant component has focus. Test the chosen safeguard after moving focus away, typing in a field, and returning to the player. If a preference is offered, verify that the saved setting is applied consistently on the next visit rather than just changing the appearance of a switch.''',
'2.2.1': '''A checkout reserves a basket for five minutes. A warning that appears only five seconds before expiry does not provide the specified warning-and-extension option, even if the Extend button works. With that option, give at least twenty seconds to act and permit extension at least ten times; test each extension and confirm that the contents of the basket remain available. Turning the limit off or permitting adjustment to at least ten times the default are other options. Assess the particular exception before relying on it. A desire to keep sessions short is not by itself proof that a time limit is essential or that the activity is a real-time event.''',
'2.2.2': '''A dashboard contains a rotating promotion and a live score feed. Treat these as separate behaviors. Automatic movement, blinking, or scrolling that starts automatically, lasts more than five seconds, and appears alongside other content requires a pause, stop, or hide mechanism unless essential. Automatically updating information presented alongside other content also needs the specified controls, without applying that five-second rule. Test whether Pause actually stops updates rather than merely freezing an animation while content continues to change underneath. The control must be reachable and usable; check keyboard operation and verify that restarting updates does not erase the user's current context.''',
'2.2.3': '''A practice exam displays elapsed time for the learner's own reference but never ends the attempt because of a countdown. That is different from adding a ten-minute extension to a deadline: this enhanced criterion removes timing as an essential part of the activity, subject to its stated exceptions. When reviewing a self-paced exercise, inspect server-side expiration as well as the visible timer. A hidden submission deadline can still prevent completion. Record what happens when the learner pauses for a long interval, returns, edits an answer, and submits. Do not treat a live auction's genuinely real-time nature as evidence that an ordinary practice form must also be timed.''',
'2.2.4': '''During a job application, a non-emergency chat invitation opens a modal and interrupts typing. A useful preference lets the applicant suppress or postpone such invitations while retaining a way to review them later. Test both choices: with suppression enabled, the invitation should not seize focus or interrupt the task; with postponement selected, it should be delivered at the chosen time. Emergency interruptions are treated differently under this criterion, so classify the actual message rather than assuming that every notification is urgent. Verify that the setting covers notifications emitted by embedded services as well as those produced by the application itself.''',
'2.2.5': '''A user is halfway through a benefits application when the authentication session expires. After signing in again, the application should restore the user's work and allow the same activity to continue without data loss. Test text fields, selected options, uploaded attachments where applicable, and the current step. Also verify that re-authentication does not silently submit the application. Protect stored drafts according to the sensitivity of the data and the shared-device context; persistence must not expose the information to the next person who uses the device. Try both an expired session and a normal sign-out and document which behaviors belong to each scenario.''',
'2.2.6': '''Before a learner begins a long form, tell them how much inactivity can lead to losing entered work, unless the data is preserved for more than twenty hours of inactivity. The warning must reflect the actual behavior: a message promising an hour is misleading if the server removes the draft after fifteen minutes. Check when the inactivity period starts, what actions reset it, and whether a saved draft survives a network interruption. This criterion concerns warning about possible data loss; a separate timing requirement can apply to the time limit itself. Do not assume that satisfying one of these requirements automatically satisfies the other.''',
'2.3.1': '''An alert animation alternates bright red and dark backgrounds four times in one second. Replace the effect with a static message and icon when the flash is not necessary. When an effect remains, evaluate the actual rendered sequence and the general and red flash thresholds with an appropriate analysis method; a still screenshot cannot establish the number or nature of flashes. Inspect embedded advertisements and video as well as first-party UI. The Level A rule allows content that does not exceed three flashes in any one-second period, or that remains below both applicable thresholds. A pause control alone does not demonstrate that the flashing content meets either condition.''',
'2.3.2': '''A small status badge flashes four times within one second. The designer argues that it occupies a tiny portion of the page, but the enhanced requirement does not retain the threshold alternative from the Level A criterion. Remove the rapid flashing and provide a persistent status label or other nonflashing feedback. Inspect the actual sequence over every one-second interval; an average across a longer animation can conceal a burst of rapid flashes. Review loading indicators, notifications, embedded content, and animations on error, including states that appear only after a network failure.''',
'2.3.3': '''Opening a drawer causes the whole page to zoom and slide. A person who experiences vestibular symptoms should be able to disable nonessential interaction-triggered motion. Test with the operating system's reduced-motion preference enabled before opening the page, and inspect any application-level setting as well. The drawer should still open, show the same controls, and put focus in an appropriate place without requiring the user to experience the unwanted transition first. Distinguish motion animation from a simple change of state, and document any claim that an animation is essential to functionality or information rather than merely attractive.''',
'2.4.1': '''On every page of a documentation site, forty navigation links precede the main article. Put a skip link early in the focus order so that a keyboard user can bypass the repeated block. Starting from the browser chrome, Tab into the page, activate the link, and then press Tab again. Check that the destination is visible, the next focus stop belongs to the main content, and a fixed header has not covered the destination. Repeat after navigating to another page. A landmark or other mechanism may also be appropriate, but simply adding an invisible link without verifying its destination and focus behavior is not a useful bypass.''',
'2.4.2': '''A browser tab labeled “Home” provides little help when it actually contains the payment step of a checkout. Change the document title to identify the current purpose, such as “Payment details — Example Store.” Open several pages in separate tabs and compare only their titles to see whether a user can distinguish them. In a single-page application, navigate through the router and use browser Back and Forward; the document title should track the current view rather than remaining on the first route. Include useful state information when it helps identify the current page, but avoid making the title so long that its distinguishing words are hidden.''',
'2.4.3': '''A form is displayed as name, address, and payment, but positive tabindex values make focus jump from payment to the page footer and back to name. Fix the underlying source order or focus management rather than adding more positive tabindex values. Trace the sequence with Tab and Shift+Tab at desktop width, narrow width, and high zoom, because a layout can rearrange visually without changing document order. When a dialog opens, moves focus, and closes, inspect the surrounding sequence as well. The criterion allows more than one logical order, but the chosen order must preserve meaning and operation.''',
'2.4.4': '''A news page has three links called “Read more,” each beside a different story. Nearby text is not automatically programmatically determined context merely because it is visually close. Inspect each link in context with a screen reader and review the surrounding structure. If the applicable context does not make the destination clear, revise the link text to identify the story, for example “Read the housing policy update.” Compare this Level A requirement with the enhanced link-purpose criterion, which examines the link text itself. Repeated names are especially confusing when links lead to different destinations.''',
'2.4.5': '''An employee guide can be reached through a deep menu, but the site search does not index it and there is no site map entry. Add another working way to locate the guide, such as an indexed search result or a meaningful site map. Verify that the second method actually reaches the same page and is usable with a keyboard, rather than merely existing in the interface. The criterion has an exception for a page that is a step in, or result of, a process. A routine help article does not become a process result simply because it is linked from an application form.''',
'2.4.6': '''A registration form uses “Details” for three unrelated field groups and “More” as the heading for several sections. Rewrite the labels to reflect their topics, such as “Delivery address” and “Payment method,” and connect each label to the appropriate control. Ask a reviewer to scan the headings and labels without reading all surrounding copy: they should be able to predict the subject or purpose. Inspect the accessibility tree to confirm that the name presented to assistive technology matches the intended label. A label can exist programmatically and still be too vague to describe the control's purpose.''',
'2.4.7': '''A custom stylesheet removes outlines from every control and relies on hover color alone. Someone navigating by keyboard cannot tell which button will activate. Add a visible focus indicator and test it against every background where the control appears, including dark mode, validation errors, and high-contrast settings. Tab through links, fields, menu items, and dialog controls; watch for clipping by overflow containers and for indicators hidden beneath sticky elements. Focus-visible styling can avoid unnecessary pointer focus decoration, but keyboard focus must remain visibly indicated. The enhanced focus appearance criterion adds separate measurable requirements and should not be conflated with this baseline criterion.''',
'2.4.8': '''A learner lands directly on an article from search and cannot tell whether it belongs to forms, navigation, or media guidance. A breadcrumb such as “Guides > Forms > Error messages” can communicate the location within the larger set. Check that the earlier items lead to the expected parent pages and that the current page is correctly identified. A current item in a navigation menu can be another location cue. Inspect the location indicator on deep links and after client-side navigation; a breadcrumb that remains on the previous route can be worse than one that is absent. Distinguish the page's title from its location among related pages.''',
'2.4.9': '''A screen reader's links list shows ten entries named “Read more,” with no surrounding article headings. For this enhanced criterion, make each link's own text identify its purpose, for example “Read the accessible forms guide.” Review links in isolation, including icon-only controls whose accessible text may be generated by an accessible name. Avoid relying only on the paragraph or table cell that surrounds the link. When multiple links have the same purpose, consistent names can be appropriate; the important distinction is whether the link text identifies where or what the link leads to.''',
'2.4.10': '''A policy article presents registration, payment, refunds, and appeals as a single uninterrupted block. Divide the material into sections and provide headings that describe the topic of each section. Use real heading elements so users can navigate the document structure, and check that the heading hierarchy reflects the relationship between main topics and subsections. A heading should prepare the reader for what follows, not merely repeat a generic phrase such as “More.” Evaluate sections that were added through a content management system, where visually bold text may not have heading semantics.''',
'2.4.11': '''A sticky footer covers a focused Submit button completely when the user tabs to the bottom of a form. Move the footer, provide sufficient scroll offset, or otherwise ensure that the component is not entirely hidden by author-created content. Test the entire keyboard path, not just a manually scrolled screenshot: focus movement can trigger a different scroll position. Repeat at high zoom, with a small viewport, and when a cookie notice or other fixed region is present. This minimum criterion concerns complete obscuration of the focused component; the enhanced criterion requires a stronger outcome.''',
'2.4.12': '''A sticky header covers the top half of a focused link. Because the link is only partly hidden, the minimum requirement and this enhanced requirement have different outcomes. For the enhanced target, adjust scroll margins or overlay placement so the entire focused component remains visible. Check each edge of the component, not merely whether a few pixels of its focus indicator appear. Repeat when text wraps, the viewport becomes shorter, or the on-screen keyboard reduces available space. If several fixed regions can appear together, test their combined effect instead of reviewing each in isolation.''',
'2.4.13': '''A button changes its border from light gray to slightly darker gray when focused. Evaluate both the indicator's area and the contrast change between the focused and unfocused states at the same pixels. A high-contrast one-pixel dot may be too small, while a broad subtle outline may not provide enough contrast change. Compare the measured area with the area of a two CSS-pixel-thick perimeter of the unfocused component and check the specified 3:1 change, taking the criterion's exceptions into account. Inspect screenshots at the same scale and ensure the indicator is not clipped by a neighboring container.''',
'2.5.1': '''A gallery moves between photographs only when the user swipes across the image. Provide Previous and Next controls that can be activated with a single pointer action without tracing a path. Verify that the buttons reach every photograph available through swiping and that their state communicates when an endpoint is reached. Multi-point gestures and path-based gestures are covered by this requirement unless the gesture is essential. Keyboard support remains valuable but does not by itself supply the single-pointer alternative required here. Test with a mouse, touch, and another pointer input where available.''',
'2.5.2': '''A delete control performs the deletion immediately on pointer down. To examine accidental activation, press the control, hold the pointer down, move outside the target, and release. A design that completes on release can allow the user to abort before completion or undo afterward, in line with the criterion's options. Check actual business effects as well as the visual button state: a server request fired on pointer down can delete the item even if the interface later looks canceled. Use reversible sample records during testing. Also distinguish a justified essential down-event from a developer's convenience.''',
'2.5.3': '''A visible button says “Search” but an aria-label overrides its accessible name to “Find products.” A speech-input user may say the visible word and be unable to target the control reliably. Include the visible text in the accessible name, preferably at the start, such as “Search products.” Inspect the computed name rather than assuming the markup combines labels as intended. Repeat after translation and when the button changes state. A longer name can add context, but replacing the visible words with a synonym defeats the connection between what a user sees and what the assistive technology can target.''',
'2.5.4': '''A mobile app clears a form when the device is shaken. Give users another interface control for the same function and let them disable motion actuation, unless an applicable exception is met. Test those protections separately: perform the operation without moving the device, then disable motion and confirm that shaking no longer triggers it. Consider accidental movements while a user is walking or using a mobility aid. A label in help documentation does not replace the alternative control or disable setting. Document the basis for any essential-function or accessibility-interface exception rather than assuming all sensor input qualifies.''',
'2.5.5': '''A toolbar shows a sixteen-pixel icon, but its clickable button includes padding and measures forty-four by forty-four CSS pixels. Measure the interactive area, not just the artwork. Check adjacent controls so enlarged hit areas do not overlap or direct an activation to the wrong action. The enhanced criterion requires a target capable of containing a 44 by 44 CSS-pixel square, subject to its listed exceptions. Test zoom, responsive layouts, and controls inside embedded components. Do not substitute the smaller target and spacing rules of the minimum criterion for this enhanced target.''',
'2.5.6': '''A tablet application detects touch at startup and disables keyboard event handlers. A user who begins with touch and then connects a keyboard cannot finish the task. Remove the restriction and test transitions among touch, keyboard, mouse, and available assistive input without reloading. Inspect embedded widgets separately because they may apply their own modality detection. The criterion permits restrictions when essential, required for security, or necessary to respect user settings; merely detecting a touch-capable screen is not enough. Document the actual reason for any restriction and check that other available inputs continue to work elsewhere.''',
'2.5.7': '''A task board lets users move cards only by dragging. Add a Move menu that allows a user to select the destination column with individual pointer activations, without holding and steering the card. Verify that the alternative can reach every destination and preserves the same result, including ordering if ordering is part of the operation. Keyboard shortcuts are useful but do not alone provide a single-pointer alternative. Test the menu with touch and a mouse, and inspect whether authorization or validation rules are consistent with the drag path. The criterion has exceptions for essential dragging and functionality determined by the user agent.''',
'2.5.8': '''Two adjacent icon buttons each have a sixteen-by-sixteen CSS-pixel hit area. Increase each target to contain a 24 by 24 CSS-pixel square, or carefully evaluate an applicable exception such as spacing. Under the spacing exception, examine the specified 24 CSS-pixel circles centered on undersized targets and whether they intersect another target or the relevant circle around another undersized target. Measure actual hit regions rather than icon artwork and repeat after responsive layout changes. Inline links, equivalent controls, user-agent-determined targets, and essential presentation have specific conditions; identify the applicable condition instead of assuming that all small controls are exempt.'''
}

FAMILY_GUIDANCE = {
'2.1': '''Keyboard testing begins before the first page control. Place focus in the browser address bar, enter the page, and use Tab and Shift+Tab to trace the sequence. For native controls, exercise Enter and Space as appropriate; for a composite widget, check its documented arrow keys, selection behavior, and exit method. Record the control, the action attempted, the result, and the point where the task stopped. Repeat after opening any menu or dialog, because its interactive state may have a separate focus sequence. A focusable element is not necessarily operable, and a click handler is not evidence that keyboard activation works.

Look at the complete user journey as well as each control. A person who can select an item but cannot confirm or cancel it still cannot complete the task. Where scripts move focus, verify that focus lands on a meaningful, visible target and remains recoverable after closing a layer. Test with the browser's native keyboard behavior before adding custom handlers, and avoid overriding established keys without a clear reason. A keyboard-only check is valuable, but it does not replace examining pointer and assistive-technology behavior under the other applicable criteria.''',
'2.2': '''When a feature depends on time, map the point at which a timer starts, the event that ends it, and the consequence for the user's task. Read the interface copy and then observe the actual server behavior; a visible countdown can differ from the expiration enforced on the server. Check what happens to entered information before expiry, at expiry, and after a return to the task. Use a test account and sample data so that losing a draft does not affect a real user. If an extension or preference is offered, check its actual effect and whether it remains understandable and operable with a keyboard.

Different timing criteria protect different outcomes. A warning about data loss is not necessarily a way to extend a time limit; a pause for moving content is not necessarily a way to resume an expired session. Apply the conditions and exceptions of the specific criterion in the lesson, rather than treating every timer as the same case. For any exception, describe the real function and why the conditions apply. Product convenience, advertising schedules, or a general preference for short sessions are not automatically proof that a time limit or interruption is essential.''',
'2.3': '''Review both the content and the conditions under which it appears. An animation may be harmless in its resting state but begin after a user opens a menu, receives an error, or changes a setting. Identify whether the issue is flashing, interaction-triggered motion, or both, then apply the corresponding criterion. A control to pause moving content addresses a different requirement and should not be assumed to establish that rapid flashes are safe. When flashing must be assessed against thresholds, use an appropriate analysis method for the rendered media; do not infer compliance from a single still image.

For motion-sensitive users, test preferences before triggering the effect and check that disabling motion leaves the underlying information and controls available. Examine first-party transitions and embedded material, including loading, success, and failure states. Document the exact state, viewport, sequence, and relevant preference used during testing so another reviewer can reproduce the observation. If a team believes an effect is essential, explain what information or function would be lost without it; visual polish alone does not establish that the animation is essential.''',
'2.4': '''Navigation testing should follow an actual task. Start at a realistic entry point, such as a search result or the browser address bar, and identify how a user finds the page, understands its purpose, reaches the relevant section, and moves among controls. Check both the visible interface and programmatically available structure: browser titles, heading elements, links, landmarks, and focus order may communicate different parts of the experience. Use a screen reader or accessibility tree inspection to verify names and structure, then perform a separate keyboard pass to observe focus and navigation.

Repeat the check after client-side route changes and at a narrow viewport or high zoom. Sticky headers, responsive reordering, hidden menus, and dynamically inserted content can change the result. Record the exact starting point and actions, the expected destination or focus location, and what actually happened. Fixing one cue does not automatically fix another: a descriptive page title does not create a bypass link, and an on-screen label does not guarantee that focus remains visible. Evaluate the particular condition and level in the lesson before deciding whether the observed behavior passes.''',
'2.5': '''Evaluate the pointer task from the user's perspective, from the initial contact through the final result. Identify whether the design requires a path, multiple contact points, dragging, a down-event, precise targeting, or movement of the device. Those are distinct interaction characteristics, and each has its own requirements and exceptions. Check that an alternative completes the same underlying operation, including any selection, confirmation, and error recovery; an alternative that opens a menu but cannot finish the task is incomplete.

Test the actual interactive region rather than only the visible icon. Use browser tools to inspect CSS-pixel dimensions and boundaries, then try adjacent targets with a mouse or touch. For cancellation, press and hold, move away, and release using sample data, and verify whether a server-side action occurred. For accessible naming, compare visible text with the computed name and repeat after localization or state changes. Keyboard access is important, but a keyboard-only workaround does not automatically meet a requirement for an alternative operable by a single pointer.'''
}

